From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:51:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 25/27] Multithreaded engine: neighbour flags instead of the
 two barriers of the fused sweep

With the fused sweep every timestep still ended at two barriers of all threads. On the Apple M5 (4 P + 6 E
cores) a thread that macOS moves to an E core, or that loses its core to another process, holds up all the
others: at 8 threads they waited 24-27 % of the time. A thread needs its neighbours only: the voltages of its
last x-line need the currents of the line after its slab, which the next thread updates (its line before the
slab), and its line before the slab needs the final voltages of the previous thread's last line.
FusedTimestepFlags() replaces the barriers by two step counters per thread (StepFlag: it spins like SpinBarrier
for OPENEMS_BARRIER_SPIN_US, then sleeps on a condition variable; seq_cst store and load, so no wake-up is
lost). Before its last block a thread waits until the next thread has done its line before the slab in the
previous timestep, then publishes its sweep; before its line before the slab it waits for the previous thread's
sweep of this timestep, then publishes that line. Every cell sees the same operands as with the barriers
(bit-identical). Neighbours are at most one timestep apart, threads t and t+k at most k.

The threads are then at different timesteps, so the extensions' line hooks must not depend on a common one:
TS_LOCAL (engine_extension.h) declares that they keep no state shared by x-lines that changes from one
timestep to the next and read the timestep through GetNumberOfTimesteps(), which returns the calling engine
thread's own count while the flags run (thread_local). Mur (fast path), UPML (vector and scalar SSE paths), the
excitation, the dispersive family (Lorentz/Drude, conducting sheets, Debye) and lumped RLC declare it. Lumped
RLC moved its [n], [n-1], [n-2] containers on by a pointer rotation once per timestep (a mutex and an atomic
stamp in its line hooks); it now takes them from the timestep itself, [k] = base[(k-ts-1) mod 3], the same
arrays as the rotation (a slot indexed by the timestep, as MrHighVoltage proposed in upstream Discussion
#492). With an extension that does not declare it (the steady-state detection, until the next commit), the
barriers stay and the verbose message names that extension; OPENEMS_FLAGS=0 keeps them too. The flags count
the timesteps of one IterateTS() call; numTS moves on after it.

Verified bit-identical against the barriers and against stock v0.37.0: bench/check.sh (six models), boxes with
Mur, PML_8, lumped RLC, conducting sheets, Drude/Lorentz and Debye blocks at 1 to 10 threads (76 runs, 48 of
them with flags), scripts/checks/debye_check.sh, and a stress run with OPENEMS_BARRIER_SPIN_US=0 (every wait
that is not satisfied at once sleeps) at 3, 8 and 10 threads and the automatic count, under a watchdog: no hang.
Paired interleaved A/B in one build, flags against OPENEMS_FLAGS=0, 5 rounds, other processes on 0.3-0.6
cores, median MC/s and median paired ratio:
  model            4 threads             8 threads
  GCPW line        1573 -> 1598 x1.016   1337 -> 1719 x1.286
  notch tutorial   1621 -> 1650 x1.015   1481 -> 1805 x1.227
  SMA launch       1263 -> 1307 x1.035   1437 -> 1590 x1.109
  patch tutorial   1644 -> 1722 x1.055   1577 -> 1702 x1.073
  patch x5         1659 -> 1708 x1.029   1802 -> 1985 x1.102
  notch x3         1694 -> 1739 x1.019   2006 -> 2081 x1.077
The per-thread timestep costs the barrier path nothing measurable (the build before runs at x0.97-1.02 of it).
OPENEMS_FUSED_STAT at 8 threads: the threads wait 10-22 % of the time (patch x5, SMA, GCPW).

